#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# root_reduce

#% text
# `root_reduce(a)` writes the algebraic number `a` as one number: a rational, `u + v sqrt(D)` when its degree is 2, or one root object `root(x => p(x), k)` with `p` its minimal polynomial.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `a` may be built from rationals, complex rationals, radicals and root objects with sums, products and powers.

#% item
# Which root of `p` is meant is decided with certified ball enclosures; the index follows the order of root objects: real roots ascending, then the others by real and imaginary part.

#% item
# An expression that is not an algebraic number stays unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A sum of square roots as one root object:

#% cell
root_reduce(sqrt(2) + sqrt(3))
#% output
#= root(&(1 - 10*&1^2 + &1^4), 4)

#% text
# Numbers of degree 2 are written with a square root:

#% cell
root_reduce((-1)^(1/3))
#% output
#= 1/2 + (im/2)*3^(1/2)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A power of a root object:

#% cell
root_reduce(root(x => x^3 + x + 1, 1)^2)
#% output
#= root(&(-1 + &1 + 2*&1^2 + &1^3), 1)

#% text
# Expressions that are rational:

#% cell
[root_reduce(sqrt(2)*sqrt(8)), root_reduce(sqrt(2) + sqrt(3) - sqrt(5 + 2*sqrt(6)))]
#% output
#= [4, 0]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The same number as its root object:

#% cell
n([root_reduce(2^(1/3) + im), 2^(1/3) + im])
#% output
#= [1.2599210498948732 + 1.0im, 1.2599210498948732 + 1.0im]

#% section
# See also

#% text
